==English==

===Etymology===
{{compound|en|desk|top}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|dĕskʹ-tŏp}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛsktɒp/}}
* {{a|AU}} {{IPA|en|[ˈdɛsk.ˌtɔp]}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|[ˈdɛsk.ˌtɒp]}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|[ˈdɛsk.ˌtɑp]}}
* {{hyphenation|en|desk|top}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The top [[surface]] of a desk.
#: {{cot|en|benchtop|tabletop}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[desktop computer]].
#: {{cot|en|laptop|palmtop}}
#: {{ux|en|Laptops are often more expensive than '''desktops'''.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A personal computer, as opposed to computers for business or server purposes.
# {{lb|en|computing|GUI}} The main [[graphical user interface]] of an [[operating system]], usually displaying [[icon]]s, [[window]]s and background [[wallpaper]].
#: {{ux|en|I installed a new application and it added its icon to my '''desktop'''.}}
#* '''1994''', Linda Mui, Valerie Quercia, ''X User Tools'' (page 312)
#*: Important menus, such as the root menu or Workspace menu, are "pinnable," which means that you can pin them up and keep them on your '''desktop''' {{...}}
# {{lb|en|computing|GUI|synecdoche}} The [[wallpaper]] of this interface.
#* {{seeCites|en}}

